= Unpacking Your FujiNet Cartridge

Congratulations on your FujiNet cartridge, one of the most exciting things
your ATARI 5200 has ever been plugged into. We suggest that you read this
Owner’s Manual thoroughly before setting it up.

When you take the FujiNet cartridge from its box, you will have:

- The FujiNet cartridge
- This Owner’s Manual

#plate(art("cart-hero", w: 1.6in))

The FujiNet cartridge goes in your console’s cartridge slot like any other
game. Inside it is a complete network adapter: a WiFi radio, a memory-card
reader and two small computers. It lets your 5200 play games that are kept on
the Internet, on a memory card, or on a computer in your home -- and play some
of them against people far away.

== What You Also Need

- *An ATARI 5200* of any model: the original four-port console, the later
  four-port, or the two-port model. FujiNet is made for *NTSC* (North
  American) consoles and television sets.
- *A 5200 controller*, plugged into *jack 1*. The FujiNet menu is worked
  with the joystick, the keypad and the side buttons.
- *Your WiFi network’s name and password.* FujiNet uses the 2.4 GHz band,
  which nearly every home router offers.

You may also want, though you do not need them to start:

- *A microSD memory card*, formatted FAT32 (most cards come that way), to
  keep games on.
- *A USB-C phone charger and cable*, for extra power. With a four-port
  console you will most likely need one; see Section 4.

#note[FujiNet makes no changes to your console. Take the cartridge out and
your 5200 is exactly as it was.]
